config PCOPY
	tristate "Parallel Copy/Move — Dynamic Lane & Channel Assignment"
	depends on BLOCK && BLK_DEV
	select PROC_FS
	help
	  PCOPY provides hardware-aware parallel file copy and move operations
	  with dynamic assignment of PCIe lanes and NVMe parallelization.

	  The engine dynamically adjusts parallelism based on:
	    - Current CPU load (scales channels inversely with system pressure)
	    - Total file count in the batch operation
	    - Device class speed constants (IDE, SATA, NVMe Gen3/4/5, USB)
	    - PCIe bandwidth ceiling (gen × lanes = max throughput)
	    - NVMe hardware queue depth (blk-mq nr_hw_queues)

	  Device speed constants (MB/s practical ceiling):
	    IDE HDD:       80     SATA HDD:     150    SAS HDD:      200
	    SATA SSD:     550     NVMe Gen3:   3500    NVMe Gen4:   7000
	    NVMe Gen5:  14000     USB 2.0:       35    USB 3.0:      400
	    USB 3.1G2:    900     USB4/TB3:    3000

	  The optimizer will not assign more parallelism than the device can
	  absorb. An HDD gets 1-2 channels; NVMe Gen5 gets up to 64.

	  Channel formula:
	    channels = min(cpu_available, hw_queues, files, device_ceiling, pcie_bw)

	  CPU load policy (normal priority):
	    Load <25%: use 80% of cores    Load <50%: use 50%
	    Load <75%: use 25%             Load >90%: use 1-2 cores

	  Exposes:
	    /proc/pcopy/status    - Hardware detection and dynamic state
	    /proc/pcopy/config    - Tunable parameters (override dynamic)
	    /proc/pcopy/decision  - Last assignment decision with reasoning
	    /dev/pcopy            - ioctl interface for pcopy/pmove tools

	  To compile as a module, choose M here: the module will be called pcopy.

	  If unsure, say M.
